The single skip is
`tests.test_mutations_checks.FixtureInventory.test_a_symlink_in_place_of_a_file_fails`,
which needs the Windows privilege to create a symbolic link and skips itself
rather than reporting a pass it did not earn.

    worker 0: 28 tests, 482.3s of test time
    worker 1: 45 tests, 396.9s of test time
    worker 2: 19 tests, 564.0s of test time
    worker 3: 33 tests, 584.1s of test time
    worker 4: 40 tests, 593.7s of test time

Per-worker test time sums to more than the elapsed wall clock because the
runner distributes individual test IDs, so a class fixture runs once per test
method rather than once per class. That is the cost of every test being
independently addressable and sharing no state.

## What this run covers that the last one did not

The fabrication export now identifies its copper layers by filename rather
than by Gerber X2 metadata, so this run exercises `FAB.LAYER_IDENTITY` and the
filename-based archive allowlist alongside the X2-based one the frozen Rev A
fixture still uses. Both paths are tested; a board on either keeps its archive
check.

One test is known to be sensitive to machine load rather than to the code:
`tests.test_runner.RunnerContract.test_concurrent_tests_never_share_an_output_directory`
asserts that four staggered tests reach more than one worker, which does not
hold when the machine is already saturated. It passes on an idle machine and
is unrelated to anything the fabrication change touches.
